fingerprint.customer:
Initial filter:

- Poland-based consumer ecommerce service covered by the Act and not within the microenterprise service exclusion;
- merchant controls the checkout component source and release process;
- one stable web stack and repository, excluding marketplace-controlled payment UI;
- recurring releases and measurable checkout volume;
- decision-maker has an accessibility, customer-support, or enforcement concern;
- willingness to pay PLN5k for a component-only pilot.

An approximate GUS upper bound is 11.2k non-micro firms reporting some electronic orders. It includes B2B, marketplace, exempt, and non-controlled experiences and is not a qualifying buyer denominator.

fingerprint.offer_and_business_model:
Covered non-micro Polish ecommerce merchants pay for a version-locked accessibility warranty on one merchant-controlled checkout form component, combining a manual assistive-technology baseline, venture-maintained fixes, first-remedy rights, and capped labour/service credits while excluding fines, lost sales, and external code changes.

The first product is a PLN5,000, 60-day paid pilot for one named repository, component, production URL, and version range:

- baseline keyboard, focus, zoom, contrast, error, label, and two-screen-reader manual test;
- defined acceptance checklist and evidence pack;
- remediation of defects inside the merchant-controlled component;
- regression harness for the agreed states;
- first-remedy right after a covered regression;
- capped remedial labour and service credits if the venture misses its fix time.

The annual renewal hypothesis is PLN12,000 for the same bounded component, version process, scheduled manual retest, and a fixed labour/credit cap.

fingerprint.payer_and_paid_event:
Hypothetical pricing and mature cost:

- PLN5k 60-day paid pilot;
- PLN12k annual component warranty;
- target annual delivery cost PLN5k–7k per customer after templates, including tester time, engineering, manual retest, support, and claim reserve;
- no cash indemnity, legal-fine coverage, revenue guarantee, or whole-site certification.

The annual fee competes with inexpensive audits and agency retainers. No observed claims data supports the target cost or margin.

fingerprint.customer:
Initial filter:

- Poland-based B2B SaaS or regulated-service operator;
- roughly PLN4m–80m annual revenue;
- one clearly isolated business-critical workload using PostgreSQL plus Kubernetes/containers;
- no mainframe, SAP, clinical production, payment-core, or multi-region active-active scope;
- customer owns application source and has authority over data and destination;
- a current buyer, lender, insurer, acquisition, renewal, or board continuity event;
- willingness to pay PLN20k for the first drill.

No public source proves the filtered denominator. Ten Polish public-cloud customer references establish examples, not a market count.

fingerprint.offer_and_business_model:
Polish mid-market B2B SaaS and regulated-service companies pay for a scoped annual cloud-exit option that maintains customer-owned exports, executable replacement infrastructure, and restoration drills for one revenue-critical workload, with the venture owing only predefined remedial labour and capped service credits—not cash indemnity for business loss.

The first paid product is a PLN20,000 fixed-scope Exit Drill for one named workload:

- customer-controlled export destination;
- dependency and excluded-provider-IP inventory;
- executable replacement infrastructure for the agreed open components;
- data restore and integrity checks;
- application start and synthetic transaction;
- measured RTO/RPO and unresolved dependency list;
- a 12-month option proposal with one scheduled repeat drill.

The venture never promises functional equivalence for source-only managed features.

fingerprint.payer_and_paid_event:
Hypothetical pricing:

- PLN20k first drill;
- PLN120k–180k annual option for a bounded workload, including one scheduled drill, monthly export-verification checks, artefact maintenance, and up to a fixed number of remedial engineering hours;
- additional migration execution priced separately and never assumed in the wealth case.

Hypothesized mature gross margin is 55% after specialist time, test infrastructure, security review, and service-credit reserve. No public comparable supports the annual price.

fingerprint.compounding_advantage:
The primary control point is the signed, paid, model-life mandate plus exclusive aftermarket licence, public order routing, and funded reserve. The authorised-representative title and the warehouse are not scarce by themselves.

Within twelve months and total founder spend of PLN 100,000 or less, control proof requires:

- one live manufacturer mandate for one named model;
- exclusive order, distribution, and permitted data rights;
- documented authentic supply, repair-information, and pairing access;
- a funded reserve or consigned stock;
- insurer-approved liability boundaries;
- enforceable term and successor protection; and
- one eligible order fulfilled and recorded through the controlled endpoint.

If counsel says the transition and model-tail protections are unenforceable, if the manufacturer can route around the endpoint, or if required supply/software rights are revocable at will, the control point fails.

For a signed model, the venture aims to control the exclusive ordering endpoint, authentic-parts distribution and conditional reproduction rights, funded reserve and stock schedule, repairer records, pairing/documentation workflows, and complete order/failure/replenishment history for the prescribed tail. Those contractual model-specific assets are the proposed switching cost.

An established authorised representative could copy the bundle for future models and begins with far more trust. The AR label, software, lifecycle model, and 3PL are not a moat. The candidate survives only if model-specific rights are enforceable for the full tail and if a small unknown entrant can repeatedly win complete packages before incumbents.

fingerprint.customer:
The beachhead is non-EU manufacturers of rugged or enterprise smartphones and slate tablets that:

- launch one to five covered models annually into the EU;
- sell a hypothesised 20,000 to 100,000 EU units per model;
- lack an integrated EU repair and lifecycle-parts network;
- have no incumbent contract already providing the complete mandate, funded reserve, supply/IP rights, ordering route, and transition protections; and
- can lawfully grant every required right.

The buyer is the manufacturer's export, legal/compliance, product, or general-management leader. Integrated brands, manufacturers unable to grant supplier/IP/software rights, and manufacturers unwilling to pre-fund the tail are excluded.

fingerprint.distribution_mechanism:
Before paid proof, founder spending is capped at PLN 25,000:

- PLN 12,000 for legal scope, mandate, licence, reserve, and termination analysis;
- PLN 4,000 for insurance eligibility and liability-structure work;
- PLN 4,000 for translated research and direct outreach;
- PLN 2,000 for a secure document-room and order-flow demonstration; and
- PLN 3,000 contingency.

The founder maps 50 exact non-EU manufacturers and their next models or renewal dates from EPREL, model documentation, brand sites, marketplaces, and company registers. A weak-looking public parts route is only an outreach signal, never an accusation. The founder sends a model-specific EU lifecycle cost-and-rights checklist and offers a fixed-scope paid admission package. Testing labs, advisers, and incumbent representatives are not assumed to be channels until contracted.

Paid proof within 180 days is a PLN 25,000 to 40,000 non-refundable onboarding payment under the complete conditional mandate. Live liability begins only after the manufacturer supplies the documentation, rights, reserve, stock plan, indemnity, and insurance conditions. A further PLN 15,000 may be spent after payment to bind cover and finish onboarding. Family capital remains excluded.

The founder can perform research, modelling, sales, and vendor coordination within about five hours daily while employed. Counsel, an insurance broker, a part-time repairability/QMS engineer, and an insured 3PL perform specialist work. A named backup handles authority contact and operational exceptions before any live mandate.

fingerprint.problem_trigger:
The painful event is an imminent EU model launch with a seven-year operational tail but no funded operator, authentic-supply schedule, repair endpoint, pairing/documentation workflow, or successor plan. The first offer is a paid model-admission package that produces one conditional mandate, lifecycle cost model, reserve schedule, public ordering design, repairer-registration workflow, authentic-parts schedule, and controlled fulfilment test.

Hypothetical commercial terms:

- PLN 25,000 to 40,000 non-refundable onboarding per model;
- PLN 4 to 7 of earned stewardship revenue per EU unit;
- PLN 20,000 to 40,000 annual minimum per active model;
- a separate PLN 4 to 10 per EU unit paid into the segregated reserve; and
- 25% to 40% gross margin on authentic-parts transactions.

The reserve is excluded from revenue. Willingness to pay fails unless two manufacturers provide written commercial indications and at least one pays onboarding under the complete conditional mandate.

disconfirming_test:
Run one bounded test, not a software launch:

1. Reconcile 100 public records across two DSOs and compare 20 against underlying project evidence.
2. Interview 15 qualified portfolio controllers and inspect how they prove delivery date, expiry, security, agreement, and escalation status.
3. Send public-data exposure cards to 50 qualified organizations.
4. Issue at least three paid proposals.
5. Obtain one 50% deposit for a PLN 12,000–20,000 reconciliation by day 120, with day 180 as the absolute paid-proof deadline.
6. Deliver up to 25 SPVs while measuring labor, errors, action rate, and contribution.
7. Seek a 12-month portfolio-wide mandate after delivery.

Disconfirm the pivot if:

- the public-to-private material matching error remains above 5% after manual verification;
- fewer than five interviewees identify an unresolved exposure worth more than the proposed fee;
- no customer pays before granting private access;
- delivery requires more than 2.5 recurring analyst hours per SPV;
- contribution margin cannot exceed 70% after repeatable delivery;
- no customer will route all in-scope SPVs through an annual mandate;
- no customer permits narrowly defined derived outcome rights;
- counsel identifies a legal/data constraint that cannot be solved within the initial budget;
- the product remains an occasional reconciliation service rather than a recurring capital-gate record.

fingerprint.compounding_advantage:
The beachhead is not “renewable software.” It is the post-2026 Polish connection-condition evidence workflow for lean multi-SPV developers.

The intended compounding loop is:

1. Public records create low-cost identification and pre-sale exposure cards.
2. Each paying portfolio supplies verified document matches and resolution outcomes.
3. The venture improves entity resolution, exception taxonomy, and expected remediation time.
4. Historical source snapshots become impossible for a later entrant to recreate retrospectively.
5. The monitoring order makes the product the continuing record for new and existing SPVs.
6. Customer-approved derived data improves benchmarks without exposing project documents.
7. Audit continuity and workflow integration raise switching cost.

A competitor can copy alerts. It cannot instantly recreate the historical changes, verified mappings, outcome labels, and client-specific audit trail. If customers refuse portfolio-wide routing or derived-data rights, this intended control point does not form.

The primary control point is the combination of:

- an owned, continuously timestamped archive of changing DSO source records;
- verified entity-resolution links and evidence provenance;
- a signed 12-month portfolio-wide monitoring mandate;
- customer adoption of the GridClock evidence packet as an internal capital-release requirement;
- explicit contractual rights to retain anonymized, non-identifying derived event and outcome labels;
- a client-specific audit history that must be migrated and revalidated if the customer switches.

Control-point proof within 12 months and PLN 100,000 requires:

- one annual order covering at least 20 SPVs;
- automatic inclusion of newly created or acquired in-scope SPVs;
- at least three capital-gate decisions or reporting cycles using the evidence packet;
- one customer-approved derived-data clause;
- a functioning archive across at least two DSO source formats;
- no reliance on an exclusive partnership or status-based introduction.

If only a public alert dashboard is purchased, the control-point proof has failed.

fingerprint.offer_and_business_model:
**Thesis:** sell an annual, client-controlled connection-condition monitoring and evidence workflow to independent Polish renewable/storage developers that manage 15–80 SPVs, beginning when conditions are issued—not when they are about to expire.

The economic buyer is the portfolio CFO, investment director, or head of development. The user is the grid/project-control team. The product reconciles public DSO events with documents already owned by the customer, maintains a timestamped evidence trail, and produces an exception packet that the customer makes mandatory before internal capital is released or a project proceeds to its next gate.

The venture becomes a single-sided workflow and data vendor:

- no SPV ownership or option fee;
- no seller sourcing;
- no buyer reservation;
- no share marketing or success-fee brokerage;
- no dependence on a closing;
- payment begins with a fixed-fee portfolio reconciliation and converts to an annual mandate.

Only projects with at least 240 days of expected validity remaining enter the initial implementation. Acute rescue remains outside scope.

fingerprint.payer_and_paid_event:
The first product contains five linked functions:

1. Weekly capture and historical archiving of relevant DSO publications.
2. Entity resolution across applicant, SPV, project location, capacity, and KRS records.
3. Client-controlled extraction of delivery dates, expiry dates, security, agreement, land, and evidence fields.
4. Exception routing with owner, due date, evidence hash, source confidence, and escalation.
5. A capital-gate evidence packet showing current status, unresolved discrepancies, and audit history.

Illustrative pricing and economics—each a hypothesis requiring paid proof:

- paid reconciliation: PLN 12,000–20,000 for up to 25 SPVs, 50% upfront;
- annual monitoring: PLN 48,000 base plus PLN 1,800 per active SPV;
- typical 35-SPV account: approximately PLN 111,000 annual revenue;
- direct data, security, support, and analyst cost: PLN 18,000–24,000 per typical account after automation;
- target mature gross margin: 78%–84%;
- direct-outreach CAC ceiling: PLN 15,000;
- target first-year CAC payback: under three months;
- required gross annual retention: at least 85%.

The founder-wealth path does not count distributions and company value twice. While the founder remains employed, 95% of after-tax distributable cash is reinvested in the conservative and expected cases; optional distributions are capped at 5% and excluded from the figures below. Strong success reinvests 100%.

| Scenario | Customers / volume | Revenue and margins | Capital and ownership | Founder-equity path |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Conservative, year 8 | 36 Poland/CEE accounts; roughly 1,200 monitored SPVs; PLN 110k average annual revenue | PLN 3.96m revenue; 82% gross margin; PLN 1.50m operating cost; about PLN 1.75m EBITDA | First-year capital capped at PLN 97k; growth funded by reinvestment; 80% founder ownership after employee/strategic pool | 4x EBITDA implies about PLN 6.99m business value and **PLN 5.59m founder equity** |
| Expected, year 7 | 65 accounts; roughly 2,600 SPVs; PLN 125k average | PLN 8.13m revenue; 85% gross margin; PLN 2.70m operating cost; about PLN 4.21m EBITDA | 80% founder ownership; no venture round required in the model | 5x EBITDA implies about PLN 21.0m value and **PLN 16.8m founder equity** |
| Strong, year 7 | 110 CEE accounts; roughly 5,500 SPVs; PLN 150k average | PLN 16.5m revenue; 87% gross margin; PLN 5.30m operating cost; about PLN 9.06m EBITDA | 75% founder ownership after growth hiring or strategic financing; 100% reinvestment while employed | 6x EBITDA implies about PLN 54.3m value and **PLN 40.7m founder equity** |

Intermediate milestones:

- month 6: one paid reconciliation;
- month 12: three unrelated annual customers and at least 60 monitored SPVs;
- year 3: 12 accounts, approximately 300 SPVs, and roughly PLN 1m ARR;
- year 5: 25–28 accounts and at least PLN 2.5m ARR;
- year 7–8: test whether the conservative customer and pricing denominator is real.

The fresh evaluator should reject the wealth path if the addressable account denominator, average price, retention, or mature gross margin cannot be evidenced without heroic CEE penetration.

fingerprint.problem_trigger:
The customer’s painful event is not merely an alert arriving late. It is discovering during investment committee, financing, acquisition, or contractor mobilisation that:

- a condition is closer to expiry than the central register showed;
- a security, agreement, land, or corporate dependency lacks evidence;
- the public record and internal data room disagree;
- management funded the next project stage using stale status;
- no auditable record shows who saw the exception and what evidence resolved it.

The willingness-to-pay hypothesis is that a portfolio owner will spend PLN 12,000–20,000 for an initial reconciliation and roughly PLN 48,000 annual base plus PLN 1,500–2,000 per monitored SPV when one avoided lapse, misallocated diligence cycle, or delayed capital decision is worth materially more. This must be tested; the statute proves urgency, not budget.

disconfirming_test:
Days 1–30:

- execute the bounded engineer arrangement and obtain counsel’s go/no-go view on the mandate and tripartite call;
- build one public-data demonstration ring and forty-buyer list;
- founder spend remains at or below PLN 12k.

Days 31–100:

- approach forty qualified private buyers;
- require one buyer to provide its measured demand envelope, sign the exclusive mandate and prepay at least PLN 20k;
- kill the pivot if every buyer demands unpaid success-only work or no buyer prepays.

Days 101–165:

- screen at least thirty sources inside the paid buyer’s envelope;
- use buyer funds for source premium and engineering;
- require one source to sign the exact tripartite conditional call with definite price, volume, availability and exercise mechanics.

By day 180:

- require the buyer to pay a second, non-refundable rights milestone of at least PLN 25k after the source call;
- total founder cash at risk remains at or below PLN 15k;
- founder remains within five hours per day.

Kill if the call can only promise future negotiation, the source refuses short compensated exclusivity, the buyer will not fund the source premium, the engineer cannot create objective conditions, or the second milestone is not paid.

fingerprint.customer:
Initial niche: privately controlled industrial-park or closed-heat-network operators in the Wielkopolska–Łódź–Mazovia corridor that:

- have an existing or board-authorised 1–5 MW low- or medium-temperature replacement load;
- can provide a measured demand curve;
- are considering boiler replacement, capacity expansion or a defined decarbonisation project;
- control their procurement budget without a municipal tender;
- can finance feasibility and later sponsor an SPV; and
- lack a dedicated external heat-source origination team.

The first source search is performed only after one such buyer pays. Public utilities, speculative developers without capital authority and buyers seeking unpaid success-only scouting are excluded from the proof phase.

Initial filter:

- independent Polish general diagnostic laboratory, excluding Diagnostyka, ALAB, Synevo, and hospital systems;
- one chemistry or immunoassay analyser placement ending within six months;
- documented monthly volume and bounded test menu;
- qualified laboratory diagnostician and existing external-quality process;
- service engineer and lawful compatible consumable route available for the exact serial;
- willingness to sign a four-year minimum and pay PLN20k before asset purchase.

KIDL lists 2,554 sites and 1,851 exact entity strings. A deliberately loose filter leaves at most 613 sites/518 normalised organisations; it is not a qualifying buyer denominator.

fingerprint.offer_and_business_model:
Independent Polish diagnostic laboratories prepay a conditional deployment deposit and sign a minimum-test lease for one serial-specific, unmodified, compliant used/open analyser with verified reagents, maintenance, and swap service, giving the venture title-based routing control without becoming the device manufacturer.

The first product covers one exact unmodified analyser serial and one defined test menu:

- PLN20k conditional deployment deposit, credited to the first contractual minimum;
- complete CE/declaration-of-conformity, UDI, instructions, history, seller, intended-use, and transport evidence;
- verified compatible reagent, calibrator, control, and quality-control route;
- documented preventive and corrective maintenance;
- swap or external-test-routing SLA with explicit exclusions;
- four-year minimum-test or minimum-monthly-payment lease.

The venture buys the serial below PLN100k only after the customer contract and evidence conditions are satisfied.

fingerprint.payer_and_paid_event:
Hypothetical mature annual revenue per installed analyser: PLN100k–140k from minimum payments and gross margin on verified reagents/service. Expected-scale economics assume:

- gross asset acquisition below PLN100k per serial;
- external lease/asset finance after the first repeat proof;
- annual fully loaded gross margin of 35%–40% after reagent cost, service, quality controls, asset-finance charge, swaps, and bad debt;
- customer minimum term of four years.

No public source supports the acquisition price or PLN140k annual revenue. One hundred placements imply about PLN10m gross asset capital before swap stock.

fingerprint.customer:
Initial filter:

- independent Polish freight-wagon keeper or lessor, not a vertically integrated large fleet;
- 20–200 wagons as a prospecting hypothesis, not a verified segment;
- one known revision/repair due within 90 days;
- selected ECM and exact maintenance-delivery workshop;
- measurable replacement-wagon, demurrage, or lost-hire exposure;
- willingness to prepay PLN12k before the venture owes the full workshop reservation.

ERA reports 71,818 Polish freight wagons and 142 ECM-certified organisations; a VKM extraction found 453 Polish keeper names. None is a qualifying buyer denominator.

fingerprint.offer_and_business_model:
Small independent Polish freight-wagon keepers and lessors pay a non-refundable option over a named maintenance-workshop date and work package, documented in a tri-party keeper–ECM–workshop contract, while the venture controls only the commercial reservation and never assumes ECM, release-to-service, or return-to-operation responsibility.

The first paid product is a PLN12,000 non-refundable priority option for:

- one identified wagon;
- one named workshop and date window;
- one predefined work package and intake requirements;
- explicit arrival, cancellation, excess-work, substitution, and refund terms;
- recognition by the keeper, its ECM, and the maintenance-delivery workshop.

The underlying maintenance is paid directly by the keeper or transparently passed through. The option does not guarantee technical release, completion after hidden damage, or network availability.

fingerprint.payer_and_paid_event:
Hypothetical economics per exercised or expired option:

- customer option fee: PLN12k, prepaid and non-refundable after conditions are satisfied;
- workshop capacity reservation: PLN4k–6k only after customer cash;
- legal, coordination, and service cost: PLN2k–3k at repeat scale;
- target contribution: PLN3k–6k before central overhead.

Maintenance, parts, transport, replacement wagon, and excess work are excluded or separately prepaid. No public slot price or margin validates these figures.
